{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>The first word: I am.</b> Know that all the commandments follow two paths. The first path: commandments implanted by HaShem in the heart of all people of understanding, and they are many. Among the Ten Words there is only the Sabbath alone that is not included under rational weighing. Therefore every intelligent person of every nation and tongue acknowledges them, for they are implanted in rational weighing, and to them one may neither add nor subtract. These are what Abraham kept, along with other additional commandments. HaShem gave the Torah only to people of understanding; whoever lacks understanding has no Torah. The second path: commandments that are concealed, and it is not explicit why they were commanded. Far be it, far be it, that any one of them should contradict rational weighing. Rather, we are obligated to keep everything HaShem has commanded us, whether the secret is revealed to us or not. If we find one of them contradicting rational weighing, it is not correct to believe in it literally. Rather, in the books of our sages of blessed memory we seek its reason if it is by way of metaphor, and if we do not find this written, we seek and search with all our ability — perhaps we can establish it. And if we cannot, we leave it as it is and acknowledge that we did not know what it was. For example: ומלתם את ערלת לבבכם — 'and you shall circumcise the foreskin of your heart' (Deuteronomy 10:16). Is it possible He commanded us to butcher ourselves like brutes? We know with clear knowledge that all the commandments that are not required by rational weighing have a single secret for each one, and if it is hidden from us — the witness to this is that for some of them it is written why they were commanded, such as the Sabbath, which is a remembrance of the act of Creation, and חג המצות — 'the Festival of Unleavened Bread' — למען תזכור — 'so that you shall remember' (Deuteronomy 16:3), and ואהבתם את הגר כי גרים הייתם — 'and you shall love the stranger, for you were strangers' (ibid. 10:19), and the prohibited mixtures — פן תקדש — 'lest you sanctify' (ibid. 22:9), and לא ירבה לו סוסים — 'he shall not multiply horses for himself' — ולא ישיב את העם — 'and he shall not return the people' (ibid. 17:16), and likewise לא ירבה לו נשים — 'he shall not multiply wives for himself' — and the reason is given: ולא יסור לבבו — 'lest his heart turn away' (ibid., ibid. 17). This is not an independent commandment, as is written in Halakhot of Shimon ben Kayra. And the reason of וקרא בו — 'and he shall read in it' (ibid., ibid. 19) is לבלתי רום לבבו מאחיו — 'so that his heart not be exalted above his brothers' (ibid., ibid. 20), and the section of הקהל — 'the assembly' — למען ילמדו — 'so that they shall learn' (ibid. 31:12). And many commandments are like this. If so, the intelligent person whose eyes HaShem has opened can know from the words of the Torah the secret of all the commandments. All the commandments fall under three categories: commandments of the heart, commandments of the tongue, and commandments of action. The commandments of the heart are of two kinds, positive and negative commandments. Positive: ואהבת את ה׳ אלהיך — 'and you shall love YHWH your Elohim' (Deuteronomy 11:1) — ולדבקה בו — 'and to cling to Him' — וליראה את השם הנכבד — 'and to fear HaShem the honored One' (ibid. 28:58) — ואהבת לרעך כמוך — 'and you shall love your fellow as yourself' (Leviticus 19:18). Negative: לא תשנא את אחיך בלבבך — 'you shall not hate your brother in your heart' — ולא תקום ולא תטור — 'you shall not take vengeance nor bear a grudge' (ibid., ibid. 17–18). The commandments of the tongue too are positive and negative. Positive, such as the Shema and the grace after meals and the priestly blessing, also the confession of tithe, and many others. Negative: לא תענה ברעך — 'you shall not answer against your fellow' (Exodus 20:13 [16]) — אלהים לא תקלל — 'Elohim you shall not curse' (Exodus 22:27 [26]) — לא תקלל חרש — 'you shall not curse the deaf' (Leviticus 19:14). Commandments of action, positive and negative, are many, and there is no need to mention them. The commandments of the heart are the principal ones, honored above all. Many thought there is no sin in thoughts of the heart, except in matters of idolatry. But in truth, this alone [the denial of HaShem] is harsher than all evil thoughts; rather, it stands against all of them. Behold it is written: שש הנה שנא השם — 'six things does HaShem hate' (Proverbs 6:16) — and there it is written לב חורש מחשבות און — 'a heart plotting iniquity' (ibid., ibid. 18). And written: הטיבות כי היה עם לבבך — 'You were pleased that it was with your heart' (II Chronicles 6:8 [דה״ב]) — הטיבה ה׳ לטובים ולישרים בלבותם — 'Do good, YHWH, to the good and to the upright in their hearts' (Psalms 125:4) — והיה לבבכם שלם — 'and let your heart be whole' (I Kings 8:61) — אני ה׳ חוקר לב — 'I, YHWH, search the heart' (Jeremiah 17:10) — וה׳ יראה ללבב — 'and HaShem sees into the heart' (I Samuel 16:7). This first word is the foundation of all the nine words written after it, and it is close to the commandments of the heart. The meaning of this word is that one should believe, and the belief of one's heart should be without doubt, that this honored Name — which is written but not pronounced — He alone is his Elohim." "<b>R. Judah Halevi of blessed memory asked me</b> why He mentioned יהוה your Elohim who brought you out of the land of Egypt, and did not say who made heaven and earth, and I made you. This was my answer to him: Know that the ranks of men are not equal in the belief of their hearts that they believe in the honored Name. For the masses believe because things have reached their ears — their master told them so. Above them are those who saw this written in the words of the Torah that HaShem gave to Moses, and if a heretic comes to challenge that there is no Elohim, they put their hand to their mouth, for they do not know how to respond. But one whose heart moves him to study the sciences — which are like rungs to ascend by to the place he desires — will recognize the deed of HaShem in metals, in plants, in animals, and in the human body itself: he will know the function of each organ according to its nature, and why it was made in this pattern. Then his heart will be raised to know the affairs of the spheres, which are the deed of HaShem in the intermediate world that stands firm. He will know the time when the sun or the moon will be darkened and by how much the sun is darkened from it, and he will know the moon — why it was darkened and what caused it. He will know all this with complete proofs in which there is no doubt. From the ways of HaShem the discerning one knows HaShem; thus Moses said: הודיעני נא את דרכיך ואדעך — 'Make me know Your ways that I may know You' (below, 33:13). Now HaShem the honored One mentioned in the first word: אנכי ה׳ אלהיך — 'I am YHWH your Elohim' — and this cannot be understood except by one who is an extraordinary sage. For I have already explained in the portion of ואלה שמות that this Name alone is that which exists without change, and besides Him there is no 'dweller forever'; 'none like Him, enthroned of old' (Psalms 55:20 [19]). He sustains the upper world by His power, and the intermediate world by the power of HaShem and His holy angels who are in the upper world, and this lower world in which we are is sustained by the power of HaShem and by the power of the two upper worlds. For the discerning person of any nation, the word אנכי ה׳ — 'I am YHWH' — would suffice, for the making of heaven and earth occurred close to five thousand years ago, and Israel alone acknowledge this, while the sages of the nations do not deny that HaShem alone made heaven and earth; they only say that HaShem acts continuously without beginning or end. Now HaShem performed signs and wonders in Egypt until Moses brought them out, to be for them an Elohim. Thus Moses said: או הנסה אלהים לבוא לקחת לו גוי — 'Or has any Elohim attempted to come and take for Himself a nation' (Deuteronomy 4:34). The reason is that HaShem did for Israel what He did not do for any nation: HaShem created the intermediate world, and it governs the lower world according to whatever is in the constellation of each nation — good or evil, thus it befalls them, for so HaShem apportioned to them. The constellation of Israel, according to their stars, was that they would remain in servitude, but HaShem in His power, for the love of the fathers, brought new signs into the lower world that were not under the governance of the intermediate world, and He brought Israel out from under the dominion of the stars to make them for Him a nation of inheritance. On this account our ancestors said: אין מזל לישראל — 'There is no constellation for Israel' (Nedarim 32a). I will further explain this by way of analogies in the portion of כי תשא. Because of the sign that HaShem did in Egypt, Moses said: אתה הראת לדעת — 'You have been shown to know' (Deuteronomy 4:35) — all saw this, the wise and the unwise, the great and the small. He also added the matter of the stand at Mount Sinai, where they heard the voice of HaShem; therefore after it: מן השמים השמיעך את קולו ליסרך — 'from heaven He made you hear His voice to instruct you' (ibid., v. 36). And He said lastly that the perfect knowledge is what a man returns to his heart until it is proven to him with proofs that HaShem alone [is Elohim]; therefore He said: וידעת היום והשיבות אל לבבך — 'And you shall know today and return it to your heart' (ibid., v. 39). David said: ואתה שלמה בני דע את אלהי אביך ועבדהו — 'And you, Solomon my son, know the Elohim of your father and serve Him' (I Chronicles 28:9 [דה״א]). Knowledge is in the heart, not in the utterance of the mouth. HaShem mentioned to the discerning one: אנכי ה׳ — 'I am YHWH' — and added אשר הוצאתיך — 'who brought you out' — so that both the discerning and the non-discerning will understand. And He said אלהיך — 'your Elohim' — for you are obligated, because I brought you out of the house of slaves, to be for Me a servant who serves Me, and to be for Me a nation and I will be for you an Elohim. Moses explained this in the section כי ישאלך בנך (Deuteronomy 6:20): the meaning of the question is why we are obligated to perform the commandments of HaShem more than all other people, and is there not one Creator for all of us. He gave three answers: first, עבדים היינו לפרעה — 'we were slaves to Pharaoh' (ibid., v. 21) — and He did this great good for us; therefore we are obligated to keep everything He commands us, even if we do not know the reason for His commandments. Second, these commandments are not for His need, but for our good, to give us life all our days (ibid., v. 24). Third: וצדקה תהיה לנו — 'and it will be righteousness for us' (ibid., v. 25) — that we be worthy to inherit the World to Come. The men of investigation found that all corporeal entities are ten, and they are general principles beyond which there is nothing higher. The first is the essence of all, and it is that which exists, while the nine are all accidents, all dependent on the first and attached to it, issuing from it — for it is like the unit in the number ten, from which all number issues, and in which all number is found, for it is the foundation. This first word that HaShem the honored One said includes all the commandments of the heart, the tongue, and action. For whoever does not believe in his heart in HaShem, no commandment is upon him. A person is obligated to mention Him every moment for His honor, for everything he does, he should do only for His honor. And he should not refrain from negative commandments except for the honor of HaShem alone — like a person who gives charity to the poor: he should not give it for the honor of the communal officer, nor because people will praise him, for then the person's own standing is higher in his eyes than the honor of HaShem who gave him wealth and preferred his money over what he could give, in order to receive reward from HaShem. Likewise one who commits a transgression in secret, because the king does not know or lest people see him and hold him in low esteem — whoever thinks this is mad, for so it is written: אם יוצר עין הלא יביט — 'He who shaped the eye, shall He not see?' (Psalms 94:9). HaShem sees in secret what people cannot see openly, for HaShem knows one's thoughts and his secret. A person fears a king of flesh and blood who may die tomorrow lest he punish him, yet does not fear the true King in whose hand is his soul, in this world and in the World to Come." "The Gaon [Saadia] composed admonitions and included all the commandments within these Ten Words, and he found [allusions to] HaShem the honored One in the five first words. I have already explained that the first word is the foundation, and upon it rests the entire structure of the commandments. After it is written: לא יהיה לך — 'you shall not have' — and the transgression of one who does not believe in HaShem is greater than the transgression of one who worships idols, for there are many who believe in HaShem yet sacrifice and offer incense to idols, like those who offer incense to the Queen of Heaven, who think they benefit them, as they said: ומן אז חדלנו לקטר למלכת השמים וגו׳ חסרנו כל — 'ever since we ceased to burn incense to the Queen of Heaven… we lack everything' (Jeremiah 44:18). And written: את ה׳ היו יראים ואת אלהיהם היו עודדים — 'they feared YHWH and served their gods' (II Kings 17:33), and likewise Naaman: בהשתחויתי בית רמון — 'when I bow down in the house of Rimmon' (II Kings 5:18). These acknowledge HaShem but associate another with Him. Therefore in this word the honored Name is written, and in the third word it is also written, for the transgression of one who swears falsely is less than idolatry; rather, he belittles HaShem openly — perhaps he did this out of anger or need, for in his heart he believes in HaShem and does not associate another with Him. In the fourth word, HaShem is written — who rested on the seventh day — and one who does work on the Sabbath denies the act of Creation, and this transgression is less than taking His name in vain. In the fifth word, HaShem is written, for the parents are partners with Him in one's formation, and if one does not honor them, it is as though he does not honor HaShem. The five remaining words are directed toward one's fellow man, and the first of them is the most severe — separating the soul from the body. What follows it is not against the body, and what follows — לא תגנוב — 'you shall not steal' — concerns property, and after it the tongue, and after that חמוד — coveting — in the heart."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra uses דבור to refer to each of the Ten Commandments; rendered 'word' throughout. The abbreviation ע״ג resolved as עבודה זרה (idolatry). The abbreviation ע״ו resolved as עבודה זרה in the phrase דברי עו״ג.", 1 "The phrase מנוחתו כבוד follows the name R. Judah Halevi and is rendered as an epithet: 'of blessed memory.' The parenthetical דה״י / דה״ב abbreviations resolved as דברי הימים (Chronicles), first (א) or second (ב).", 2 "The verb עודדים in the II Kings 17:33 quotation appears as היו עודדים in the source text; the Masoretic text reads עבדים. Transmitted hyper-literally as it stands in the commentary manuscript tradition."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T20:27:50.168545087Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}